 the Chu & Stuckey corpus (§2.8, scaling only, item 06)\n3571:### (a) The optimum: the line holds to 125, the formula's constants do not, and no interval survives 100\n3635:### (b) The nodes: exponential to 125, at rates the campaign overstates by 0.01–0.02 per customer\n3814:## 15. The differential harness: is every refutation sound under relabelling? (plan 2 §2.1(a), loop0003 item 01)\n3873:### The run: every refutation sound, 88 false answers at the optimum\n3907:### The mechanism: two sound rules composed into a cycle\n3975:### The relabelling spread (item 04's noise floor), refutation at optimum − 1\n4022:### The re-covering, on 43,935 instances instead of §13's 1,400\n4097:## 16. The campaign upward: does the ridge law hold at 50–100, and at what rate? (plan 2 §2.2, loop0003 item 02)\n4208:### (a) The rate per density, and its drift with n\n4282:### (b) The ridge at 50–75, and the half ratio\n4315:### (c) The optimum at 50–75\n4323:### (d) The 100-customer sample and the price\n4362:### (e) The revised 125 × 125 prediction\n4462:## 17. DRAT proofs at n ≤ 40: can every certified optimum carry a refutation a third party can check? (plan 2 §2.1(b), loop0003 item 03)\n4542:### (a) The run\n4548:### (b) By size band\n4557:### (c) By the width k = optimum − 1, and the n × k grid\n4598:### (d) Affordability against n, and what five times the budget buys\n4654:### (e) Against the complete customer search\n4668:### (f) Preprocessing among the checked proofs\n4731:## 18. The relabelling portfolio: is label noise a free speed-up? (plan 2 §2.4, loop0003 item 04)\n4771:### (a) The refutation: the spread does not grow, and the portfolio wins nothing\n4810:### (b) The witness search: labels matter, and it is the cheap side\n4839:### (c) No cheap statistic of a labelling predicts its cost\n4852:### (d) The race: sixteen labellings of the censored `Random-100-100-2` instances, for real\n4880:### (e) What changed: the `better_move` fix costs more the larger the instance\n4910:#